    seoTitle: "DOE Directs $420M for Mid-Atlantic Subsea HVDC Offshore Grid | Choseno",
    metaDescription: "The US Department of Energy directs $420M to build shared subsea HVDC transmission cables for offshore wind farms.",
    tweet: "The US Department of Energy allocates $420M to construct shared subsea HVDC transmission cables connecting Mid-Atlantic offshore wind to the regional grid.",
    tweetarticle: "The US Department of Energy and Energy Secretary Jennifer Granholm have finalized $420 million in federal infrastructure grants to construct high-voltage direct current (HVDC) subsea transmission interties connecting 6 gigawatts of offshore wind off New Jersey, New York, and Delaware directly to the regional electric grid.\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Directs $420M in Grid Resilience and Innovation Grants (GRIP) authorized under the Bipartisan Infrastructure Law.\n- Constructs 200 miles of buried subsea 320-kV HVDC transmission cables along the outer continental shelf.\n- Builds 2 coastal converter stations in Atlantic City, NJ, and Wilmington, DE, stepping DC power down to standard AC for local distribution.\n- Eliminates redundant individual transmission lines, reducing coastal seabed dredging by 65% and saving ratepayers an estimated $1.2B in interconnection costs.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- Federal Energy Regulators & Grid Operators: Emphasize that coordinated subsea HVDC backbone transmission is essential to prevent coastal grid bottlenecks and stabilize energy prices during summer peak demand.\n- Coastal Communities & Commercial Fisheries: Acknowledge the reduced cable footprint while demanding rigorous ongoing electromagnetic field monitoring to ensure zero disruption to benthic marine ecosystems and scallop fisheries.\n\nCHOSENO — GOOGLE REVIEWS FOR DEMOCRACY & POLICY:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for democracy. Review public decisions, track government accountability, and share your rating on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/doe-allocates-420-million-for-mid-atlantic-subsea-hvdc-offshore-wind-transmission-cables-2026-08-25\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/doe-allocates-420-million-for-mid-atlantic-subsea-hvdc-offshore-wind-transmission-cables-2026-08-25\n\n#DOE #JenniferGranholm #OffshoreWind #EnergyGrid #CleanEnergy #Infrastructure #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "WASHINGTON — Energy Secretary Jennifer Granholm and the Department of Energy's Grid Deployment Office announced on Monday the allocation of $420 million from the Bipartisan Infrastructure Law's Grid Resilience and Innovation Partnerships (GRIP) Program to construct a shared subsea high-voltage direct current (HVDC) transmission backbone connecting multiple offshore wind generation facilities off the Mid-Atlantic coast directly into the PJM Interconnection regional power grid.\n\nThe project addresses one of the most significant engineering challenges facing the American offshore wind sector: the inefficiency and environmental disruption of requiring every individual offshore wind developer to lay separate radial transmission cables across sensitive coastal wetlands and beachheads.\n\nA Unified Subsea Transmission Highway\nThe $420 million federal award finances:\n- 200 Miles of Subsea HVDC Cable: Armored 320-kV transmission lines buried 6 to 10 feet beneath the seabed along designated outer continental shelf corridors from New York Bight to Delaware Bay.\n- Dual Coastal Converter Substation Terminals: Constructing heavy converter stations in Atlantic County, New Jersey, and New Castle County, Delaware, converting direct current back into alternating current for regional substation injection.\n- PJM Grid Interconnection Upgrades: Modernizing circuit breakers, telemetry monitoring, and smart grid automation across coastal utility sub-stations.\n\n\"Offshore wind is a foundational pillar of America's clean energy future, but getting that clean power from ocean turbines into American living rooms requires 21st-century transmission infrastructure,\" Secretary Granholm said in a statement. \"By building a coordinated subsea HVDC grid backbone, we are cutting down on environmental disruption, dramatically reducing connection costs for consumers, and ensuring our regional electric grid remains resilient against extreme weather.\"\n\nEconomic and Environmental Balancing\nGrid engineers at PJM Interconnection and clean energy analysts commended the coordinated transmission approach, noting that shared HVDC cables prevent localized power surges and reduce overall transmission loss over long distances by more than 30% compared to traditional alternating current lines.\n\nCoastal tourism associations and maritime shipping authorities voiced support for reducing the number of shore landings from ten separate cable trenches to two centralized substations, while commercial fishing coalitions urged the Department of Energy to maintain permanent acoustic and magnetic monitoring stations along the buried routes."
