    tweetarticle: "Transportation Secretary Pete Buttigieg and the Federal Transit Administration have awarded $380 million in federal transit grants to construct dedicated zero-emission Bus Rapid Transit (BRT) corridors and deploy 350 battery-electric articulated buses across 14 mid-sized American transit agencies.\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Directs $380M through the FTA Low or No Emission Vehicle Program and Capital Investment Grants.\n- Purchases 350 high-capacity 60-foot electric articulated transit buses equipped with fast-charging pantographs.\n- Funds 65 miles of dedicated bus-only center lanes and smart traffic signal priority (TSP) intersections.\n- Constructs 14 high-power depot charging hubs powered by on-site battery storage microgrids.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- USDOT & Urban Commuters: Highlight that dedicated electric BRT corridors deliver light-rail style frequency at one-third of the capital cost, cutting transit commute times by 35% and eliminating tailpipe emissions in underserved neighborhoods.\n- Transit Budget Analysts: Welcome the capital grants while advising local transit authorities to negotiate long-term battery replacement warranties and ensure recurring municipal operating funds keep service frequencies high.\n\nCHOSENO — GOOGLE REVIEWS FOR DEMOCRACY & POLICY:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for democracy. Review public decisions, track government accountability, and share your rating on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/fta-awards-380-million-for-electric-bus-rapid-transit-zero-emission-corridors-2026-08-25\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/fta-awards-380-million-for-electric-bus-rapid-transit-zero-emission-corridors-2026-08-25\n\n#USDOT #PeteButtigieg #FTA #PublicTransit #ElectricBuses #Transportation #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "WASHINGTON — Transportation Secretary Pete Buttigieg and Federal Transit Administration (FTA) Administrator Nuria Fernandez announced on Monday the distribution of $380 million in federal competitive grants to deploy 350 heavy-duty battery-electric articulated buses, construct dedicated center-running bus lanes, and install high-power megawatt depot charging hubs across fourteen mid-sized transit systems in twelve states.\n\nThe awards, funded through the FTA's Low or No Emission Grant Program and the Capital Investment Grants (CIG) program, target fast-growing metropolitan transit systems in cities including Albuquerque, Grand Rapids, Richmond, Spokane, and Tucson.\n\nTransforming Bus Lines into High-Speed Transit Corridors\nBus Rapid Transit (BRT) systems utilize dedicated roadway lanes, off-board fare payment kiosks, and automated traffic signal priority to provide transit speeds and passenger capacities comparable to light rail systems at a fraction of the capital construction cost and timeline.\n\nKey components of the $380 million federal allocation include:\n- 350 American-Made Battery-Electric Buses: Zero-emission 60-foot articulated buses featuring high-energy density nickel-manganese-cobalt battery packs manufactured under strict Buy America standards.\n- 65 Miles of Transit Priority Lanes: Red-painted center lanes and signal pre-emption hardware allowing buses to bypass highway and arterial traffic gridlock.\n- Depot Microgrids and Resilient Charging: 150-kW to 450-kW fast-charging overhead pantographs paired with stationary battery energy storage systems to avoid local utility demand surcharges during peak evening hours.\n\n\"Public transportation is about freedom—the freedom to get to work, school, healthcare, and community life reliably and affordably,\" Secretary Pete Buttigieg said during the announcement. \"With these awards, we are modernizing transit across America's mid-sized cities, delivering modern, quiet, zero-emission buses that run on dedicated lanes so riders spend less time sitting in traffic and more time doing what matters to them.\"\n\nTransit labor unions, urban planning associations, and environmental justice coalitions praised the awards, pointing out that electrifying heavily trafficked transit routes substantially reduces diesel particulate exposure in dense residential neighborhoods."
