    seoTitle: "Interior Directs $310M for Colorado River Canal Lining | Choseno",
    metaDescription: "The Department of the Interior allocates $310M to line agricultural canals and protect Lake Mead water elevations.",
    tweet: "The Department of the Interior allocates $310M to line earthen canals and conserve 400,000 acre-feet of water in Lake Mead.",
    tweetarticle: "Interior Secretary Deb Haaland and the Bureau of Reclamation have committed $310 million in federal water resilience funding to line earthen irrigation canals and conserve 400,000 acre-feet of water in Lake Mead across Arizona, California, and Nevada.\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Directs $310M from the Inflation Reduction Act's Western Drought and Water Conservation Fund.\n- Installs synthetic geomembrane and concrete liners across 120 miles of agricultural canals, preventing 15% seepage losses.\n- Deploys acoustic water telemetry meters and automated sluice gates across Lower Colorado River irrigation districts.\n- Retains 400,000 acre-feet of water in Lake Mead, keeping reservoir levels above critical hydroelectric generation thresholds.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- Federal Water Officials & Southwestern Cities: Emphasize that aggressive canal efficiency prevents mandatory emergency water shutoffs for 40 million municipal residents and safeguards Hoover Dam power generation.\n- Agricultural Water Districts: Commend federal funding for infrastructure upgrades while stressing that long-term voluntary conservation payments must remain fair as basin states negotiate post-2026 river guidelines.\n\nCHOSENO — GOOGLE REVIEWS FOR DEMOCRACY & POLICY:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for democracy. Review public decisions, track government accountability, and share your rating on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/interior-department-directs-310-million-for-colorado-river-canal-lining-and-lake-mead-conservation-2026-08-25\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/interior-department-directs-310-million-for-colorado-river-canal-lining-and-lake-mead-conservation-2026-08-25\n\n#Interior #DebHaaland #ColoradoRiver #WaterConservation #LakeMead #Drought #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "WASHINGTON — Secretary of the Interior Deb Haaland and Bureau of Reclamation Commissioner Camille Calimlim Touton announced on Monday the commitment of $310 million in federal drought mitigation capital from the Inflation Reduction Act to line 120 miles of earthen agricultural irrigation canals, install acoustic flow-monitoring sensors, and preserve 400,000 acre-feet of water elevation in Lake Mead.\n\nThe investments fund long-term water efficiency projects across the Lower Colorado River Basin, partnering with agricultural water conservation districts in the Imperial Valley of California, the Gila River Indian Community in Arizona, and the Southern Nevada Water Authority.\n\nEliminating Canal Seepage and Protecting Hydropower Elevations\nIn arid southwestern environments, unlined earthen canals lose up to 15% to 20% of their diverted water volume to subterranean soil seepage and rapid evaporation before reaching agricultural crop fields.\n\nThe $310 million federal program finances:\n- 120 Miles of Heavy Geomembrane & Concrete Canal Liners: Eliminating water loss along major distribution laterals across the Imperial and Yuma agricultural districts.\n- Automated Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition (SCADA) Gates: Replacing manually adjusted weir boards with automated solar-powered sluice gates adjusting flows based on real-time soil moisture telemetry.\n- System Conservation Agreements: Securing compensated voluntary reductions that leave 400,000 acre-feet of conserved water in Lake Mead to protect the Hoover Dam's hydroelectric turbine operating intake buffers.\n\n\"The Colorado River Basin is experiencing the most severe aridification in twelve hundred years, and protecting this vital lifeline requires unprecedented collaboration and smart investments in water efficiency,\" Secretary Deb Haaland said during an announcement in Las Vegas. \"By lining critical canals and modernizing irrigation technology, we are helping agricultural producers grow their crops with less water while keeping critical storage in Lake Mead to protect 40 million people across the West.\"\n\nWestern water resource managers and tribal leaders commended the infrastructure-first approach, noting that permanent structural conservation generates recurring water savings year after year without permanently retiring prime agricultural farmland."
