    seoTitle: "Ron DeSantis Directs $180M for Wastewater Storm Surge Hardening | Choseno",
    metaDescription: "Florida Governor Ron DeSantis awards $180M to elevate controls and protect 22 coastal wastewater plants from hurricane surge.",
    tweet: "Governor Ron DeSantis allocates $180M to harden 22 coastal wastewater treatment plants against hurricane storm surge and flooding.",
    tweetarticle: "Florida Governor Ron DeSantis and the Florida DEP have directed $180 million in state resilience grants to elevate electrical switchgear, build protective seawalls, and install backup power systems at 22 coastal wastewater treatment plants vulnerable to hurricane storm surge.\n\nReview Ron DeSantis on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/ron-desantis-governor\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Directs $180M through the state-funded Resilient Florida Grant Program.\n- Hardens 22 municipal wastewater treatment facilities in Pinellas, Lee, Collier, and Monroe Counties.\n- Elevates electrical pump panels and digital control telemetry 10 feet above projected 100-year storm surge levels.\n- Prevents catastrophic sewage overflows into Tampa Bay, Charlotte Harbor, and residential canal communities during major hurricanes.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- State Leadership & Coastal Mayors: Emphasize that hardening critical sanitation infrastructure protects public health, prevents multi-million dollar post-hurricane repairs, and safeguards marine water quality.\n- Environmental Watchdogs: Praise plant elevation while urging the state to ban new residential development in low-lying coastal flood velocity zones.\n\nNOW YOU HAVE THE SAY — CHOSENO:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for politicians. Don't just watch decisions happen from the sidelines — now you have the say. Review Ron DeSantis's record, speak your mind, and let your fellow constituents know where you stand on his official public wall:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/ron-desantis-governor\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/florida-governor-ron-desantis-directs-180-million-for-coastal-wastewater-storm-surge-hardening-2026-08-25\n\n#Florida #RonDeSantis #ResilientFlorida #HurricaneResilience #Infrastructure #PublicSafety #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "TALLAHASSEE, Fla. — Governor Ron DeSantis and Florida Department of Environmental Protection (DEP) Secretary Shawn Hamilton announced on Monday the distribution of $180 million in state resilience funding through the Resilient Florida Grant Program to elevate critical electrical controls, construct marine sheet-pile seawalls, and install elevated diesel generators across twenty-two coastal municipal wastewater treatment plants across the state.\n\nThe grants target facilities along vulnerable low-lying coastal estuaries in Tampa Bay, Southwest Florida, and the Florida Keys that have historically experienced severe saltwater inundation and power loss during Category 4 and 5 hurricane landfalls, leading to raw sewage overflows into public waterways and residential neighborhoods.\n\nEngineering Flood-Proof Sanitation Systems\nThe $180 million package finances:\n- Electrical Switchgear and Telemetry Elevation: $95 million to relocate electrical transformers, variable frequency drives, and SCADA control rooms onto reinforced concrete platforms 10 feet above Base Flood Elevation (BFE).\n- Perimeter Seawalls and Backflow Check Valves: $50 million installing heavy steel sheet-pile floodwalls and non-return tidal valves preventing saltwater backflow into treatment basins.\n- Elevated Emergency Backup Power: $35 million for elevated dual-fuel generators capable of maintaining full continuous primary and secondary treatment during multi-week regional power grid blackouts.\n\n\"Florida's coastal communities must be prepared for the impacts of severe weather, and that starts with protecting critical infrastructure that keeps our water clean and our neighborhoods safe,\" Governor Ron DeSantis said during an announcement in Fort Myers. \"By investing $180 million to harden twenty-two wastewater facilities against storm surge, we are preventing environmental contamination, protecting public health, and ensuring our communities can recover quickly after major storms.\"\n\nCoastal county commissioners, public works directors, and marine conservation organizations welcomed the funding, pointing out that preventing wastewater spills during hurricanes directly protects Florida's multi-billion dollar tourism, recreational fishing, and coastal real estate economies."
