    seoTitle: "Kathy Hochul Directs $160M for Adirondack & Catskill Clean Water | Choseno",
    metaDescription: "New York Governor Kathy Hochul awards $160M to modernize wastewater plants and protect Adirondack and Catskill drinking watersheds.",
    tweet: "Governor Kathy Hochul allocates $160M in clean water grants to modernize municipal wastewater plants across Adirondack and Catskill communities.",
    tweetarticle: "New York Governor Kathy Hochul and the Environmental Facilities Corporation have allocated $160 million in state clean water grants to construct advanced wastewater treatment plants and replace failing septic systems across 35 rural communities in the Adirondack and Catskill watersheds.\n\nReview Kathy Hochul on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/kathy-hochul\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Directs $160M from the Clean Water Infrastructure Act and the Environmental Bond Act.\n- Modernizes 18 municipal wastewater treatment plants with advanced membrane bioreactors.\n- Replaces 1,200 aging shoreline residential septic tanks with centralized vacuum sewer connections.\n- Eliminates phosphorus and nitrogen runoff into Lake George, Lake Champlain, and the NYC drinking water reservoir watershed.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- State Leadership & Environmental Coalitions: Highlight that upgrading small rural treatment systems protects pristine lakes, halts toxic blue-green algae blooms, and safeguards drinking water for millions of New Yorkers.\n- Upstate Town Supervisors: Commend the state grants for covering up to 80% of project capital costs, preventing crushing sewer rate spikes on small rural property tax bases.\n\nNOW YOU HAVE THE SAY — CHOSENO:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for politicians. Don't just watch decisions happen from the sidelines — now you have the say. Review Kathy Hochul's record, speak your mind, and let your fellow constituents know where you stand on his official public wall:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/kathy-hochul\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/governor-kathy-hochul-allocates-160-million-for-adirondack-and-catskill-municipal-wastewater-modernization-2026-08-25\n\n#NewYork #KathyHochul #CleanWater #Adirondacks #Catskills #Environment #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "ALBANY, N.Y. — Governor Kathy Hochul and New York State Environmental Facilities Corporation (EFC) President and CEO Maureen A. Coleman announced on Monday the awarding of $160 million in state clean water grants to upgrade eighteen municipal wastewater treatment plants, construct centralized collection mains, and replace failing private septic systems across thirty-five upstate communities throughout the Adirondack Park and Catskill Mountain watershed regions.\n\nThe awards, financed through the Clean Water Infrastructure Act and the voter-approved Clean Water, Clean Air, and Green Jobs Environmental Bond Act, resolve a major environmental and economic challenge for rural towns: small property tax bases cannot independently fund multi-million dollar wastewater engineering overhauls required to protect high-priority drinking watersheds.\n\nEliminating Nutrient Pollution in Upstate Waterways\nThe $160 million allocation provides:\n- 18 Municipal Plant Upgrades: $105 million for advanced tertiary biological nutrient removal (BNR) and ultra-violet disinfection systems removing 95% of phosphorus and nitrogen discharges.\n- Centralized Shoreline Sewer Extensions: $35 million replacing decaying private septic tanks along pristine recreational lakes with vacuum sewer mains connected to modern treatment plants.\n- Septic Replacement Assistance Grants: $20 million providing up to $10,000 per homeowner for rural residents in sensitive watershed zones to install high-performance engineered septic drainfields.\n\n\"Clean water is a fundamental right, and protecting our state's most iconic natural treasures in the Adirondacks and Catskills requires targeted investments in modern municipal infrastructure,\" Governor Kathy Hochul said during an announcement in Lake Placid. \"By directing $160 million in state grants, we are taking the financial burden off rural taxpayers, eliminating harmful algal blooms in our lakes, and safeguarding drinking water supplies for millions of New Yorkers.\"\n\nEnvironmental advocacy organizations including The Adirondack Council and Riverkeeper commended the funding, noting that eliminating nutrient pollution preserves cold-water brook trout habitats and supports upstate outdoor recreation economies."
