    seoTitle: "JB Pritzker Directs $140M for Illinois EV Battery Recycling | Choseno",
    metaDescription: "Illinois Governor JB Pritzker awards $140M to build commercial EV battery recycling and cathode plants in Normal and Decatur.",
    tweet: "Governor JB Pritzker allocates $140M in state REV grants to build EV battery recycling and cathode manufacturing hubs in Illinois.",
    tweetarticle: "Illinois Governor JB Pritzker and the Department of Commerce and Economic Opportunity have allocated $140 million through the REV Illinois program to build commercial closed-loop EV battery recycling and cathode manufacturing facilities in Normal and Decatur.\n\nReview JB Pritzker on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/jb-pritzker\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Directs $140M in capital matching funds through the Reimagining Energy and Vehicles (REV Illinois) Act.\n- Constructs 2 commercial hydrometallurgical recycling plants processing 30,000 tons of end-of-life battery packs annually.\n- Recovers 95% of critical battery minerals, including lithium, nickel, and cobalt, for direct reuse in new EV manufacturing.\n- Creates 1,400 permanent high-wage advanced manufacturing and chemical engineering jobs in Central Illinois.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- State Leadership & Industrial Manufacturers: Highlight that closed-loop battery recycling creates a self-sustaining Midwestern supply chain, slashing EV production costs and carbon footprints.\n- Environmental Watchdogs: Commend mineral recycling while demanding strict air emissions permits and wastewater treatment standards for industrial hydrometallurgical processing.\n\nNOW YOU HAVE THE SAY — CHOSENO:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for politicians. Don't just watch decisions happen from the sidelines — now you have the say. Review JB Pritzker's record, speak your mind, and let your fellow constituents know where you stand on his official public wall:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/jb-pritzker\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/governor-jb-pritzker-directs-140-million-for-ev-battery-recycling-and-cathode-reprocessing-in-illinois-2026-08-25\n\n#Illinois #JBPritzker #ElectricVehicles #BatteryRecycling #Manufacturing #Economy #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "NORMAL, Ill. — Governor JB Pritzker and Illinois Department of Commerce and Economic Opportunity (DCEO) Director Kristin Richards announced on Monday the awarding of $140 million in state manufacturing tax credits and capital grants through the Reimagining Energy and Vehicles (REV Illinois) incentive framework to construct two commercial battery recycling plants and cathode precursor manufacturing hubs in Normal and Decatur.\n\nThe investment establishes a closed-loop automotive supply chain in Central Illinois, enabling automotive manufacturers to recycle battery packs from electric buses, delivery vans, and passenger vehicles directly within the state, recovering battery-grade critical minerals without relying on foreign mining and refining operations.\n\nCircular Economy for Electric Vehicle Manufacturing\nThe $140 million state incentive package finances:\n- Closed-Loop Hydrometallurgical Recycling Plants: $85 million for zero-thermal hydrometallurgical processing equipment extracting battery-grade lithium carbonate, nickel sulfate, and cobalt sulfate at 95% purity.\n- Cathode Precursor Synthesis Facility: $40 million to manufacture engineered cathode powders directly feeding nearby Midwestern EV assembly plants.\n- Hazardous Materials Transport Safety & Training: $15 million partnering with Heartland Community College and Illinois fire protection districts for battery handling safety.\n\n\"Illinois is leading the nation in building a comprehensive, sustainable electric vehicle ecosystem from assembly lines to component recycling,\" Governor JB Pritzker said during a ceremony in Normal. \"By investing $140 million to build state-of-the-art battery recycling facilities in Central Illinois, we are creating over a thousand high-wage manufacturing jobs, reducing carbon emissions, and securing our domestic supply of critical minerals.\"\n\nBuilding trades unions, regional economic development corporations, and automotive industry leaders commended the state's aggressive clean manufacturing policy, noting that co-locating battery recycling alongside vehicle assembly lines cuts freight logistics expenses by over 25%."
