    seoTitle: "Josh Shapiro Directs $120M for Lead Pipe Removal in PA Schools | Choseno",
    metaDescription: "Pennsylvania Governor Josh Shapiro allocates $120M to remove lead water pipes and install filtered stations in 450 schools.",
    tweet: "Governor Josh Shapiro allocates $120M in state grants to replace lead water service lines across 450 Pennsylvania public schools.",
    tweetarticle: "Pennsylvania Governor Josh Shapiro and PENNVEST have allocated $120 million in state clean drinking water grants to replace lead service lines and install certified filtration hydration stations across 450 public schools in Philadelphia, Pittsburgh, and Erie.\n\nReview Josh Shapiro on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/josh-shapiro\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Directs $120M in non-repayable grants through the Pennsylvania Infrastructure Investment Authority (PENNVEST).\n- Replaces aging lead utility connection lines and interior lead-soldered plumbing in 450 school buildings.\n- Installs 6,000 NSF-certified lead-reducing bottle refill stations.\n- Guarantees zero-detectable lead drinking water for over 320,000 public school students across the Commonwealth.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- State Leadership & Parent-Teacher Coalitions: Point out that lead exposure causes permanent neurological and developmental harm, making complete plumbing eradication in older schools an urgent moral obligation.\n- Education Budget Watchdogs: Commend full grant funding while urging ongoing independent third-party water testing at every school water outlet twice annually.\n\nNOW YOU HAVE THE SAY — CHOSENO:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for politicians. Don't just watch decisions happen from the sidelines — now you have the say. Review Josh Shapiro's record, speak your mind, and let your fellow constituents know where you stand on his official public wall:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/josh-shapiro\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/governor-josh-shapiro-directs-120-million-for-lead-water-pipe-removal-in-pennsylvania-schools-2026-08-25\n\n#Pennsylvania #JoshShapiro #CleanWater #PublicSchools #Health #Infrastructure #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "HARRISBURG, Pa. — Governor Josh Shapiro and the Pennsylvania Infrastructure Investment Authority (PENNVEST) announced on Monday the distribution of $120 million in state clean water grants to excavate and replace lead service connection pipes, remediate interior lead-soldered plumbing, and install over 6,000 point-of-use certified lead filtration bottle filling stations across 450 public school facilities throughout Philadelphia, Pittsburgh, Erie, Allentown, and Scranton.\n\nThe statewide remediation program addresses older public school facilities constructed before the 1986 Safe Drinking Water Act lead plumbing ban, eliminating lead neurotoxin exposure risks for more than 320,000 elementary and secondary students.\n\nTotal Remediation Architecture for School Drinking Water\nThe $120 million PENNVEST allocation funds:\n- Service Line Excavation and Replacement: $65 million for trenchless vacuum excavation replacing legacy municipal lead service laterals with solid copper water mains.\n- Interior Plumbing and Valve Abatement: $35 million replacing legacy brass fixtures, lead-soldered copper joints, and galvanized drinking fountains in older school buildings.\n- Filtered Bottle Refill Stations & Testing: $20 million installing 6,000 automated bottle filling stations with digital filter life monitors and supporting mandatory annual ICP-MS laboratory water testing.\n\n\"Every child in Pennsylvania deserves to learn in a safe, healthy environment, and that begins with knowing that the water coming out of the school water fountain is clean and free of dangerous lead,\" Governor Josh Shapiro said during an announcement at an elementary school in Philadelphia. \"By directing $120 million in state PENNVEST grants directly to our school districts, we are fixing legacy plumbing, protecting our kids' health, and getting stuff done for the Commonwealth.\"\n\nPediatric health advocates, teachers' unions, and school board directors commended the comprehensive funding, emphasizing that 100% state grant coverage ensures under-resourced urban and rural school districts do not need to redirect instructional classroom budgets toward emergency plumbing repairs."
