    seoTitle: "Gretchen Whitmer Directs $150M for Great Lakes Port Upgrades | Choseno",
    metaDescription: "Michigan Governor Gretchen Whitmer awards $150M to modernize commercial docks and breakwaters on the Great Lakes.",
    tweet: "Governor Gretchen Whitmer allocates $150M in state maritime grants to modernize commercial docks and breakwaters across Great Lakes ports.",
    tweetarticle: "Michigan Governor Gretchen Whitmer and MDOT have directed $150 million in state maritime infrastructure grants to modernize commercial docks, install electric container cranes, and reinforce deepwater breakwaters in Detroit, Port Huron, and Sault Ste. Marie.\n\nReview Gretchen Whitmer on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/gretchen-whitmer\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Directs $150M through the Michigan Maritime and Port Modernization Grant Program.\n- Reconstructs heavy commercial loading piers at the Port of Detroit and Lake Huron shipping terminals.\n- Replaces diesel container cranes with zero-emission electric ship-to-shore gantry cranes.\n- Hardens harbor breakwaters against extreme Great Lakes water level fluctuations and winter ice pack scouring.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- State Leadership & Maritime Logistics Executives: Highlight that modernizing Great Lakes ports increases freight efficiency by 30%, takes 120,000 heavy commercial trucks off Michigan highways annually, and reduces shipping carbon emissions.\n- Environmental Watchdogs: Commend clean electric crane adoption while emphasizing that harbor dredging must follow strict sediment containment to protect Great Lakes fish spawning beds.\n\nNOW YOU HAVE THE SAY — CHOSENO:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for politicians. Don't just watch decisions happen from the sidelines — now you have the say. Review Gretchen Whitmer's record, speak your mind, and let your fellow constituents know where you stand on his official public wall:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/gretchen-whitmer\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/governor-gretchen-whitmer-directs-150-million-for-great-lakes-commercial-port-and-breakwater-hardening-2026-08-25\n\n#Michigan #GretchenWhitmer #GreatLakes #Maritime #Transportation #Infrastructure #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "DETROIT — Governor Gretchen Whitmer and the Michigan Department of Transportation (MDOT) announced on Monday the distribution of $150 million in state maritime infrastructure matching grants through the Michigan Port Modernization Program to reconstruct commercial deepwater berths, install zero-emission electric ship-to-shore container cranes, and reinforce navigation breakwaters at seven commercial ports across Lake Michigan, Lake Huron, and Lake Superior.\n\nThe investment modernizes Michigan's vital maritime supply chains, which transport iron ore, limestone, agricultural grains, and automotive components across the Great Lakes-St. Lawrence Seaway system.\n\nModernizing the Great Lakes Maritime Supply Chain\nThe $150 million state allocation funds:\n- Port of Detroit Intermodal Container Berths: $65 million to construct reinforced steel pile quays and install high-speed electric gantry cranes handling containerized freight.\n- Sault Ste. Marie & Lake Superior Breakwaters: $45 million rebuilding storm-damaged breakwaters and navigational lock approaches at Sault Ste. Marie and Marquette.\n- Port Huron & Saginaw Cold Storage Terminals: $40 million for refrigerated temperature-controlled agricultural transfer warehouses and shore-power charging plugs for commercial vessels.\n\n\"Michigan is defined by our Great Lakes, and our commercial ports are the economic engines that connect our farmers, manufacturers, and workers to global commerce,\" Governor Gretchen Whitmer said during an announcement at the Port of Detroit. \"By investing $150 million to modernize our docks, electrify our cranes, and reinforce our harbors, we are creating thousands of good-paying jobs, taking heavy freight trucks off our highways, and strengthening Michigan's economic competitiveness.\"\n\nGreat Lakes maritime carrier associations, building trades unions, and international logistics executives commended the funding, noting that marine freight transport emits 70% less greenhouse gas per ton-mile than long-haul heavy highway trucking."
