    seoTitle: "Jay Inslee Directs $170M for Hybrid-Electric Puget Sound Ferries | Choseno",
    metaDescription: "Washington Governor Jay Inslee awards $170M to construct 2 hybrid-electric 144-car ferries for Puget Sound routes.",
    tweet: "Governor Jay Inslee allocates $170M to construct two 144-car hybrid-electric ferries and shore-charging plugs for Puget Sound.",
    tweetarticle: "Washington Governor Jay Inslee and WSDOT have allocated $170 million in state Climate Commitment Act funding to construct two 144-car hybrid-electric passenger ferries and install rapid megawatt shore-charging terminals on Puget Sound.\n\nReview Jay Inslee on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/jay-inslee\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Directs $170M from Washington's cap-and-invest Climate Commitment Act auction proceeds.\n- Constructs 2 new 144-car Olympic-class hybrid-electric vessels at a Washington shipyard.\n- Installs 12-megawatt rapid automated shore-charging arms at the Seattle, Bainbridge Island, and Bremerton ferry terminals.\n- Cuts diesel fuel consumption by 19 million gallons annually, reducing Washington State Ferries' total greenhouse emissions by 40%.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- State Leadership & Maritime Engineers: Emphasize that replacing 50-year-old aging diesel ferries restores reliable ferry schedules, eliminates chronic route cancellations, and cuts operating fuel expenses in half.\n- Commuter Coalitions: Strongly support new vessel procurement while demanding WSDOT expand marine apprentice staffing programs to prevent crew-shortage delays.\n\nNOW YOU HAVE THE SAY — CHOSENO:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for politicians. Don't just watch decisions happen from the sidelines — now you have the say. Review Jay Inslee's record, speak your mind, and let your fellow constituents know where you stand on his official public wall:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/jay-inslee\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/washington-governor-jay-inslee-directs-170-million-for-hybrid-electric-puget-sound-ferry-construction-2026-08-25\n\n#Washington #JayInslee #PugetSound #CleanEnergy #Ferries #Transportation #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "OLYMPIA, Wash. — Governor Jay Inslee and Washington State Department of Transportation (WSDOT) Secretary Roger Millar announced on Monday the allocation of $170 million in state funding derived from the Climate Commitment Act to award contracts for two new 144-car hybrid-electric Olympic-class passenger-vehicle ferries and install 12-megawatt automated shore-charging arms at key ferry terminals on Puget Sound.\n\nThe investment represents the next major milestone in the Washington State Ferries (WSF) Long Range Plan, addressing widespread service disruptions caused by an aging diesel fleet where multiple 50-year-old vessels have faced extended emergency drydock repairs.\n\nElectrifying America's Largest Ferry System\nThe $170 million package finances:\n- 2 Hybrid-Electric Olympic-Class Vessels: $120 million for two 144-car vessels featuring lithium-ion battery banks paired with backup ultra-low-sulfur diesel generators, designed to operate 100% on electric battery power during standard crossings.\n- High-Power Automated Shore Charging: $35 million installing automated robotic pantograph charging arms at Colman Dock in Seattle, Bainbridge Island, and Bremerton, delivering rapid 12-MW battery recharges during standard 15-minute passenger loading windows.\n- Shipyard Workforce Training: $15 million supporting maritime building trades apprenticeships in naval architecture, marine electrical engineering, and high-voltage battery maintenance.\n\n\"Washington operates the largest ferry system in the nation, and our ferries are a critical marine highway for millions of residents and businesses,\" Governor Jay Inslee said during an announcement at Colman Dock in Seattle. \"By investing $170 million to build brand-new, clean hybrid-electric vessels right here in Washington shipyards, we are restoring reliable ferry service for island and peninsula commuters while drastically cutting diesel pollution in our waters.\"\n\nCommuter advocacy groups, Puget Sound environmental organizations, and maritime labor unions praised the procurement, emphasizing that modern hybrid vessels slash operating maintenance costs and provide a quiet, zero-emission transit experience for riders."
