    tweetarticle: "Boston Mayor Michelle Wu and City Council have authorized $60 million from the municipal capital improvement fund to install commercial rooftop solar arrays and modern geothermal heat pumps across 20 Boston Public School buildings.\n\nReview Michelle Wu on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/michelle-wu\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Directs $60M through the City of Boston Green New Deal for Public Schools initiative.\n- Retrofits 20 aging public school facilities in Dorchester, Roxbury, Mattapan, and East Boston.\n- Installs 8 megawatts of rooftop solar and replaces legacy oil/steam boilers with electric heat pumps.\n- Slashes school building utility operating expenses by $3.8M annually while providing modern air conditioning for classrooms during heatwaves.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- City Leadership & Teachers' Unions: Highlight that clean heating and ventilation improve classroom air quality, reduce student asthma absences, and ensure classrooms remain cool in early autumn.\n- Municipal Taxpayer Advocates: Support energy efficiency upgrades while urging the city to maintain strict contractor oversight to prevent construction delays during the academic year.\n\nNOW YOU HAVE THE SAY — CHOSENO:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for politicians. Don't just watch decisions happen from the sidelines — now you have the say. Review Michelle Wu's record, speak your mind, and let your fellow constituents know where you stand on his official public wall:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/michelle-wu\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/mayor-michelle-wu-allocates-60-million-for-boston-public-schools-rooftop-solar-and-heat-pumps-2026-08-25\n\n#Boston #MichelleWu #PublicSchools #GreenNewDeal #CleanEnergy #Education #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "BOSTON — Mayor Michelle Wu, Boston Public Schools Superintendent Mary Skipper, and the Boston City Council announced on Monday the authorization of a $60 million municipal capital appropriation under the city's Green New Deal for Public Schools to execute comprehensive clean energy retrofits—including commercial rooftop solar arrays, geothermal heat pumps, and dedicated outdoor air ventilation systems—across twenty school buildings in Dorchester, Roxbury, Mattapan, and East Boston.\n\nThe investment targets older masonry school facilities where legacy steam boilers and inadequate ventilation have contributed to indoor air quality challenges and forced classroom closures during severe late-summer heatwaves.\n\nDecarbonizing and Cooling Public School Classrooms\nThe $60 million municipal package funds:\n- Rooftop Solar PV Arrays: $25 million installing 8 megawatts of commercial solar panels paired with battery storage systems, supplying up to 70% of each school's annual electrical demand.\n- Geothermal & Variable Refrigerant Flow Heat Pumps: $25 million replacing 40-year-old fossil fuel boilers with high-efficiency electric heat pumps providing year-round heating and continuous classroom air conditioning.\n- High-Efficiency Energy Recovery Ventilators (ERV): $10 million installing continuous outdoor fresh air circulation systems equipped with MERV-13 air filtration.\n\n\"Every young person in Boston deserves a modern, healthy, and climate-resilient learning space where they can thrive, regardless of their zip code,\" Mayor Michelle Wu said during an announcement at an elementary school in Dorchester. \"By investing $60 million in solar energy and modern heat pumps for twenty school buildings, we are lowering energy bills for taxpayers, improving indoor air quality for our kids, and proving that climate action starts in our public schools.\"\n\nThe Boston Teachers Union, parent advocacy groups, and local environmental justice coalitions commended the municipal funding, pointing out that providing reliable air conditioning in older schools ensures equitable learning environments across all neighborhoods."
