    seoTitle: "Diane Lebouthillier Allocates $240M for Atlantic Salmon Habitat | Choseno",
    metaDescription: "DFO and Minister Diane Lebouthillier allocate $240M to build automated fish ladders and restore 60 wild Atlantic salmon rivers.",
    tweet: "Fisheries Minister Diane Lebouthillier announces $240M to restore spawning rivers and build automated fish passage ladders across Atlantic Canada.",
    tweetarticle: "Fisheries and Oceans Minister Diane Lebouthillier and Prime Minister Justin Trudeau have announced a $240 million federal conservation initiative to remove river migration barriers and build automated fish passage ladders across 60 wild Atlantic salmon rivers in Atlantic Canada and Quebec.\n\nReview Diane Lebouthillier on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/diane-lebouthillier\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Directs $240M from the renewed Canadian Oceans Protection Plan and the Nature Smart Climate Solutions Fund.\n- Restores 1,800 kilometers of upstream river habitat across New Brunswick, Nova Scotia, Newfoundland and Labrador, and Quebec.\n- Constructs 45 automated nature-like fish bypass channels and acoustic counting weirs at legacy hydroelectric dams.\n- Partners directly with 22 Mi'kmaq and Wolastoqey First Nations communities for co-managed river monitoring and temperature sensors.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- Federal Government & Indigenous River Stewards: Emphasize that wild Atlantic salmon are a vital cultural and ecological keystone species facing climate warming and habitat fragmentation, requiring urgent large-scale watershed restoration.\n- Hydroelectric Utilities: Support fish passage upgrades while emphasizing that dam modifications must maintain regional baseload hydroelectric power generation during winter peak demand.\n\nNOW YOU HAVE THE SAY — CHOSENO:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for politicians. Don't just watch decisions happen from the sidelines — now you have the say. Review Diane Lebouthillier's record, speak your mind, and let your fellow constituents know where you stand on his official public wall:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/diane-lebouthillier\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/fisheries-minister-diane-lebouthillier-allocates-240-million-for-atlantic-salmon-habitat-and-fish-ladders-2026-08-25\n\n#Canada #DianeLebouthillier #JustinTrudeau #DFO #AtlanticSalmon #Fisheries #Environment #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "OTTAWA — Minister of Fisheries, Oceans and the Canadian Coast Guard Diane Lebouthillier and Prime Minister Justin Trudeau announced on Monday the deployment of $240 million in federal funding through Fisheries and Oceans Canada (DFO) and the Oceans Protection Plan to execute comprehensive habitat restoration and automated fish passage engineering across sixty wild Atlantic salmon river systems in New Brunswick, Nova Scotia, Newfoundland and Labrador, and Quebec.\n\nThe investment targets critical historical choke points—including legacy municipal culverts, obsolete logging dams, and industrial run-of-the-river hydroelectric weirs—that have blocked wild Atlantic salmon and sea-run trout from reaching cold-water headwater spawning grounds for decades.\n\nRestoring River Connectivity for Atlantic Salmon\nThe $240 million federal initiative finances:\n- 45 Automated Nature-Like Fish Bypass Channels: $125 million constructing rock-ramp fishways and bio-engineered side-channels allowing returning adult salmon and outgoing smolts to bypass dams safely.\n- Riverbed Gravel Restoration & Thermal Pool Creation: $65 million restoring sediment balance, excavating deep thermal cold-water refuge pools, and stabilizing riverbank erosion along the Miramichi, Restigouche, and Margaree Rivers.\n- Indigenous Co-Management & Environmental Monitoring: $50 million partnering with twenty-two First Nations to deploy acoustic telemetry arrays, automated sonar counters, and water temperature data loggers.\n\n\"Wild Atlantic salmon are an essential part of Canada's heritage, supporting our ecosystems, our coastal communities, and Indigenous cultural traditions for millennia,\" Minister Diane Lebouthillier said during an announcement in Gaspé. \"By investing $240 million in river restoration and automated fish ladders, we are taking decisive action with our Indigenous and provincial partners to ensure wild salmon populations rebound for future generations.\"\n\nConservation groups including the Atlantic Salmon Federation, indigenous chiefs, and angling tourism associations welcomed the funding, pointing out that restoring wild fish stocks supports thousands of eco-tourism and guiding jobs in rural maritime communities."
