    seoTitle: "Sean Fraser Directs $350M for Transit Modular Housing | Choseno",
    metaDescription: "Housing Minister Sean Fraser allocates $350M to fund municipal infrastructure unlocking 15,000 modular homes near transit.",
    tweet: "Housing Minister Sean Fraser allocates $350M in federal infrastructure funding to unlock 15,000 modular homes near transit stations.",
    tweetarticle: "Housing Minister Sean Fraser has allocated $350 million from the federal Housing Accelerator Fund to construct municipal water mains and electrical hookups unlocking 15,000 factory-built modular homes within 800 meters of passenger rail stations across Canada.\n\nReview Sean Fraser on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/sean-fraser\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Directs $350M through the Canada Mortgage and Housing Corporation (CMHC) Housing Accelerator Fund.\n- Funds municipal utility upgrades (water mains, storm sewers, electrical substation taps) for 30 transit-oriented housing sites.\n- Unlocks the rapid construction of 15,000 factory-built modular apartment units in Ontario, BC, Alberta, and Quebec.\n- Mandates that funded developments include at least 35% deeply affordable and non-profit co-op units.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- Federal Leadership & Housing Economists: Highlight that pairing rapid modular construction with transit hub infrastructure cuts homebuilding delivery times from 4 years to 18 months, boosting housing supply where workers need it most.\n- Municipal Fiscal Watchdogs: Commend utility funding while urging the federal government to monitor local municipal permitting speed so construction starts occur without administrative delays.\n\nNOW YOU HAVE THE SAY — CHOSENO:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for politicians. Don't just watch decisions happen from the sidelines — now you have the say. Review Sean Fraser's record, speak your mind, and let your fellow constituents know where you stand on his official public wall:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/sean-fraser\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/housing-minister-sean-fraser-allocates-350-million-for-transit-oriented-modular-housing-infrastructure-2026-08-25\n\n#Canada #SeanFraser #HousingAffordability #ModularHousing #TransitOriented #CMHC #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "OTTAWA — Minister of Housing, Infrastructure and Communities Sean Fraser announced on Monday the distribution of $350 million from the federal Housing Accelerator Fund (HAF) to finance municipal enabling infrastructure—including high-capacity water mains, district stormwater retention vaults, and electrical substation feeder connections—that directly unlocks the construction of 15,000 factory-built modular homes on land parcels within 800 meters of rapid transit stations across thirty Canadian municipalities.\n\nThe investment addresses a critical bottleneck in the Canadian housing crisis: while modular construction manufacturers can produce complete apartment suites in climate-controlled factories in weeks, developments are frequently stalled for years by outdated municipal underground water, sewer, and electrical capacity around transit corridors.\n\nUnlocking High-Density Transit Housing\nThe $350 million federal allocation finances:\n- Municipal Enabling Infrastructure Grants: $210 million for water distribution mains, sanitary sewer expansions, and hydro grid interties directly servicing transit-oriented housing parcels.\n- Factory Tooling & Standardized Modular Production: $90 million in low-cost capital for Canadian modular builders utilizing standardized CMHC-pre-approved building blueprints.\n- Pedestrian Rail Connections and Public Plazas: $50 million constructing all-weather covered walkways, secure bike storage, and pedestrian plazas connecting modular apartment complexes directly into commuter train stations.\n\n\"To solve Canada's housing crisis, we need to build more homes, build them faster, and build them near reliable public transit where people can get to work without sitting in gridlock,\" Minister Sean Fraser said during an announcement in Halifax. \"By providing $350 million to build the essential underground pipes and power lines needed for 15,000 modular homes, we are breaking down barriers, supporting innovative Canadian builders, and getting shovels in the ground faster.\"\n\nMayors across the country, non-profit housing providers, and urban planning experts commended the infrastructure-first funding model, pointing out that municipal utility subsidies enable developers to build affordable rental housing without passing off-site utility costs onto tenants."
