    seoTitle: "David Eby Directs $175M for BC Rural Helipads & Paramedics | Choseno",
    metaDescription: "BC Premier David Eby awards $175M to build 16 hospital trauma helipads and modern paramedic stations across rural BC.",
    tweet: "Premier David Eby allocates $175M to construct 16 hospital trauma helipads and paramedic ambulance bases across rural BC.",
    tweetarticle: "British Columbia Premier David Eby and Health Minister Adrian Dix have allocated $175 million in provincial capital funding to construct 16 certified hospital trauma helipads and build modern paramedic response stations across Vancouver Island and the BC Interior.\n\nReview David Eby on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/david-eby\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Directs $175M through BC Emergency Health Services (BCEHS) capital allocations.\n- Constructs 16 Transport Canada-certified night-vision hospital rooftop and ground helipads.\n- Upgrades 12 rural ambulance stations with dedicated advanced life support (ALS) crew quarters.\n- Cuts medical evacuation transit times for critically injured patients in remote communities from 3 hours down to 45 minutes.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- Provincial Leadership & Paramedic Unions: Emphasize that dedicated helipads and modernized paramedic stations ensure rural and First Nations residents receive the same rapid emergency trauma care as urban centers.\n- Healthcare Budget Advocates: Strongly praise the emergency investments while urging the Ministry of Health to continue aggressive recruitment of permanent critical care flight paramedics.\n\nNOW YOU HAVE THE SAY — CHOSENO:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for politicians. Don't just watch decisions happen from the sidelines — now you have the say. Review David Eby's record, speak your mind, and let your fellow constituents know where you stand on his official public wall:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/david-eby\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/premier-david-eby-directs-175-million-for-vancouver-island-and-interior-rural-trauma-helipads-and-paramedic-bases-2026-08-25\n\n#BCPoli #DavidEby #AdrianDix #Healthcare #EmergencyServices #BCHealth #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "VICTORIA, B.C. — Premier David Eby and Minister of Health Adrian Dix announced on Monday the allocation of $175 million in provincial capital funding through BC Emergency Health Services (BCEHS) to construct sixteen Transport Canada-certified hospital trauma helipads, modernize twelve rural ambulance response stations, and deploy dedicated advanced life support medevac teams across Vancouver Island, the Kootenays, and Northern BC.\n\nThe investment targets critical emergency medical transport delays in remote and mountainous communities where patients suffering severe cardiac events, stroke, or highway trauma previously faced multi-hour road ambulance transfers over winding mountain passes before reaching specialized regional trauma centers in Victoria, Kelowna, and Prince George.\n\nTransforming Rural Critical Care Response\nThe $175 million provincial package finances:\n- 16 Certified Hospital Helipads: $85 million constructing night-vision-equipped, heated, all-weather helipads directly adjacent to emergency departments in communities including Campbell River, Port Alberni, Trail, Revelstoke, and Williams Lake.\n- 12 Modernized Paramedic Stations: $55 million replacing aging rural ambulance garages with modern facilities featuring decontamination rooms, secure pharmaceutical storage, and dedicated crew rest quarters.\n- Medevac Flight Avionics & Telemetry: $35 million equipping dedicated BCEHS critical care helicopters with satellite emergency communications and onboard blood transfusion warmers.\n\n\"When an emergency strikes in a rural or remote community, every minute counts, and people deserve to know that help is on the way fast,\" Premier David Eby said during an announcement in Nanaimo. \"By investing $175 million to build sixteen hospital helipads and upgrade ambulance stations across Vancouver Island and the Interior, we are ensuring people in every corner of British Columbia have access to fast, life-saving emergency care when they need it most.\"\n\nParamedics Association of BC representatives, rural municipal mayors, and First Nations health authorities welcomed the provincial funding, noting that direct hospital helipads eliminate intermediate ground ambulance transfers and significantly improve patient survival rates in critical trauma cases."
