    seoTitle: "Valérie Plante Directs $85M for Montreal REV Bike Network | Choseno",
    metaDescription: "Montreal Mayor Valérie Plante awards $85M to expand the protected REV bike expressway network by 45km across 6 boroughs.",
    tweet: "Mayor Valérie Plante allocates $85M to build 45km of new protected REV bike expressways and pedestrian plazas across Montreal.",
    tweetarticle: "Montreal Mayor Valérie Plante and City Council have allocated $85 million to construct 45 kilometers of new protected Réseau Express Vélo (REV) bike expressways and permanent pedestrianized plazas across six Montreal boroughs.\n\nReview Valérie Plante on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/valerie-plante\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Directs $85M through the City of Montreal Active Mobility and Urban Safety Capital Program.\n- Expands the protected REV network by 45 kilometers along Henri-Bourassa, Jean-Talon, and Côte-des-Neiges.\n- Installs concrete barrier islands, dedicated bicycle traffic signals, and heated crossing paths for winter maintenance.\n- Transforms 5 major commercial street segments into permanent seasonal pedestrian plazas with urban tree canopies.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- City Administration & Urban Transit Advocates: Highlight that dedicated, physical-separated bike expressways have doubled active cycling commutes, reduced traffic fatalities to record lows, and boosted local retail shopping.\n- Business Merchant Associations & Motorist Groups: Support cyclist safety but express concern over the removal of curbside delivery parking spots and urge the city to add reserved commercial loading zones.\n\nNOW YOU HAVE THE SAY — CHOSENO:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for politicians. Don't just watch decisions happen from the sidelines — now you have the say. Review Valérie Plante's record, speak your mind, and let your fellow constituents know where you stand on his official public wall:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/valerie-plante\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/mayor-valerie-plante-allocates-85-million-for-montreal-rev-bike-expressway-and-pedestrian-plazas-2026-08-25\n\n#Montreal #ValériePlante #REV #ActiveTransit #UrbanPlanning #Quebec #Transportation #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "MONTRÉAL — Mayor Valérie Plante and Executive Committee Member responsible for transportation and mobility Sophie Mauzerolle announced on Monday the approval of an $85 million municipal capital allocation to expand Montreal's flagship Réseau Express Vélo (REV) protected cycling expressway network by forty-five kilometers and construct five permanent pedestrian plazas across six boroughs including Rosemont–La Petite-Patrie, Côte-des-Neiges–Notre-Dame-de-Grâce, and Ahuntsic-Cartierville.\n\nThe expansion focuses on major arterial cross-town corridors—specifically Boulevard Henri-Bourassa, Rue Jean-Talon, and Chemin de la Côte-des-Neiges—connecting dense residential neighborhoods directly to Montreal's central business district, university campuses, and metro stations.\n\nDesigning All-Weather Active Mobility Corridors\nThe $85 million municipal package finances:\n- 45 Kilometers of Physically Separated Bike Expressways: $50 million constructing wide, uni-directional and bi-directional asphalt lanes separated from vehicular traffic by raised concrete curbs, bollards, and bioswales.\n- Dedicated Bicycle Traffic Signals & Intersection Safety: $20 million installing synchronized bike traffic lights providing early green-light phases and eliminating right-turn vehicle conflict points.\n- Year-Round Snow Clearing Infrastructure & Plazas: $15 million purchasing specialized narrow broom snowplows and creating five landscaped pedestrian plazas with permeable stone pavers and urban greenery.\n\n\"Montreal is recognized internationally as North America's cycling capital, and our vision is to make sure every resident, from young children to seniors, can move around our city safely and efficiently all year long,\" Mayor Valérie Plante said during a press conference on Boulevard Henri-Bourassa. \"By investing $85 million to expand our REV network and build vibrant pedestrian plazas, we are creating a greener, safer, and more accessible city for all Montrealers.\"\n\nUrban mobility associations, public health professionals, and neighborhood community groups praised the expansion, pointing out that existing REV corridors on Rue Saint-Denis have logged over 2 million trips annually while revitalizing street-level retail vitality."
