    seoTitle: "Jared Polis Directs $135M for Front Range Passenger Rail | Choseno",
    metaDescription: "Colorado Governor Jared Polis awards $135M to fund engineering and passenger passing sidings on the Front Range Rail corridor.",
    tweet: "Governor Jared Polis allocates $135M to fund engineering, passing sidings, and station platforms for Colorado Front Range Passenger Rail.",
    tweetarticle: "Colorado Governor Jared Polis and the Front Range Passenger Rail District have directed $135 million in state transit capital to advance final engineering, passenger passing sidings, and platform construction connecting Fort Collins, Denver, and Pueblo.\n\nReview Jared Polis on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/jared-polis\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Directs $135M from state transportation revenue and clean transit enterprise funds.\n- Funds final civil engineering and environmental impact reviews along 180 miles of the I-25 rail corridor.\n- Constructs 4 high-speed passenger bypass sidings on BNSF and Union Pacific trackage to eliminate freight delays.\n- Positions Colorado to unlock $1.5B in federal matching funds from the Federal Railroad Administration (FRA).\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- State Leadership & Commuter Coalitions: Highlight that passenger rail along the Front Range provides a reliable alternative to gridlocked Interstate 25, cutting highway congestion and vehicle ozone pollution.\n- Fiscal Conservatives: Urge the state to ensure final operating cost models are sustainable and do not require ongoing municipal sales tax subsidies.\n\nNOW YOU HAVE THE SAY — CHOSENO:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for politicians. Don't just watch decisions happen from the sidelines — now you have the say. Review Jared Polis's record, speak your mind, and let your fellow constituents know where you stand on his official public wall:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/jared-polis\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/colorado-governor-jared-polis-allocates-135-million-for-front-range-passenger-rail-engineering-2026-08-25\n\n#Colorado #JaredPolis #FrontRangeRail #PassengerRail #Denver #Transportation #Infrastructure #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "DENVER — Governor Jared Polis and the Front Range Passenger Rail District (FRPRD) announced on Monday the allocation of $135 million in state transit investment capital to complete final track engineering, construct dedicated passenger bypass sidings, and build multimodal station platforms along the 180-mile Front Range corridor connecting Fort Collins, Boulder, Denver, Colorado Springs, and Pueblo.\n\nThe investment advances Colorado's highest-priority intercity transit project, resolving track congestion along shared commercial freight corridors operated by BNSF Railway and Union Pacific Railroad.\n\nEngineering Intercity Rail for the Front Range Mega-Region\nThe $135 million state allocation finances:\n- 4 Dedicated Passenger Passing Sidings: $65 million constructing 2-mile high-speed bypass track segments allowing passenger trains to overtakes heavy freight consists at 79 mph.\n- Environmental NEPA and Advanced Preliminary Engineering: $40 million completing federal environmental reviews required to unlock over $1.5 billion in FRA Corridor Identification matching grants.\n- Multimodal Station Design & Platform Construction: $30 million designing downtown transit stations integrated with local bus rapid transit and regional cycling trail networks in Loveland, Longmont, and Castle Rock.\n\n\"Colorado is growing, and we need modern transportation options that keep people moving without forcing everyone to sit in bumper-to-bumper traffic on I-25,\" Governor Jared Polis said during an announcement at Denver Union Station. \"By investing $135 million to finalize engineering and build passenger rail sidings, we are bringing fast, reliable passenger rail service one major step closer to reality for millions of Coloradans from Fort Collins to Pueblo.\"\n\nMayors across the Front Range, business alliances, and environmental organizations commended the state's rail investment, emphasizing that intercity rail connects regional workforce hubs while curbing air pollution in the Denver metro ozone non-attainment area."
