    body: "PHILADELPHIA — Mayor Cherelle Parker, Police Commissioner Kevin Bethel, and City Council leadership announced on Monday the authorization of a $75 million municipal capital funding package to build ten neighborhood police storefront mini-stations, deploy two hundred newly trained walking beat officers, and install five hundred smart commercial corridor safety cameras across North Philadelphia, Kensington, West Philadelphia, and South Philadelphia.\n\nThe investment represents a core pillar of Mayor Parker's Community-First Public Safety Strategy, shifting municipal policing away from reactive vehicle patrols toward high-visibility, walking beat officers permanently assigned to specific commercial corridors and neighborhood blocks.\n\nRebuilding Community-Centric Policing Infrastructure\nThe $75 million municipal package funds:\n- 10 Neighborhood Police Storefront Substations: $35 million renovating commercial storefronts into accessible community police stations equipped with citizen reporting desks, community meeting rooms, and public Wi-Fi.\n- Walking & Bike Beat Patrol Operations: $25 million funding specialized equipment, lightweight e-bikes, and tactical gear for 200 dedicated corridor patrol officers.\n- Corridor Lighting & Smart Camera Network: $15 million installing high-intensity pedestrian street lighting and 500 optical zoom cameras connected directly into the Real Time Crime Center.\n\n\"Every Philadelphian has the right to feel safe walking down their street, shopping at their local corner store, and sending their children to the playground,\" Mayor Cherelle Parker said during a press conference on 52nd Street in West Philadelphia. \"By investing $75 million to build neighborhood substations and put 200 officers on foot patrol in our commercial corridors, we are taking back our streets, supporting our small businesses, and restoring pride and safety to every neighborhood in Philadelphia.\"\n\nNeighborhood business associations, community development corporations, and civic leaders commended the neighborhood-based policing model, pointing out that consistent foot patrols build positive trust between officers and community residents."
